POTS = C("pots", "POTS", "🧍",
    "Free POTS quiz: 12 questions on postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome: diagnosis, the stand test, salt and fluids, exercise and treatments.",
    "Twelve questions, easy to hard, on postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome: how it’s diagnosed, who gets it, and what actually helps.",
    [
        Q("What defines POTS in adults?", "Heart rate rises 30+ beats a minute within 10 minutes of standing, without a big BP drop", ["Blood pressure over 160", "Fainting at least once", "A heart rate over 50 at rest"],
          "In teenagers (12 to 19) the rise needs to be 40 or more. Symptoms must be present for at least 3 months."),
        Q("What simple test can a GP do to check for POTS?", "An active stand test (lie, then stand for 10 minutes)", ["Chest X-ray", "Blood glucose", "A treadmill test"],
          "Measure heart rate and blood pressure lying, then at 1, 3, 5 and 10 minutes standing. Leaning against a wall (the NASA lean test) works too."),
        Q("Who most commonly gets POTS?", "Women aged 15 to 50", ["Men over 70", "Babies", "Only athletes"],
          "About 4 in 5 are women. It often starts after a viral illness, surgery or pregnancy."),
        Q("Which recent illness has triggered many new cases?", "COVID-19", ["Chickenpox", "Tetanus", "Hay fever"],
          "POTS is one of the most common patterns in long COVID."),
        Q("What are the first-line lifestyle treatments?", "More fluids and salt (if blood pressure allows)", ["Fluid restriction", "Strict bed rest", "A low-salt diet"],
          "Often 2 to 3 litres of fluid a day plus extra salt. Check with a doctor if you have high blood pressure or kidney disease."),
        Q("Which compression garments help most?", "Waist-high or abdominal compression", ["Ankle socks", "Wrist bands", "A neck collar"],
          "Blood pools in the belly and legs. Squeezing the abdomen helps most."),
        Q("How should exercise be started?", "Lying or seated: rowing, recumbent bike or swimming", ["Standing sprints", "Avoid exercise forever", "Hot yoga"],
          "Upright exercise worsens symptoms at first. Gradual reconditioning helps many people improve."),
        Q("Which conditions often go along with POTS?", "Hypermobility (hEDS) and mast cell activation", ["Gout", "Kidney stones", "Glaucoma"],
          "Ask about joint hypermobility, easy bruising, flushing and food reactions."),
        Q("When are symptoms usually worse?", "In heat, after big carb-heavy meals, and in the morning", ["Only at night", "Only in winter", "Never after meals"],
          "Smaller meals, staying cool, and sitting before standing can help."),
        Q("Which is a medicine sometimes used for POTS?", "Ivabradine or midodrine", ["Furosemide", "Insulin", "Salbutamol"],
          "Low-dose propranolol and fludrocortisone are also used. Choice depends on the main symptoms."),
        Q("Is POTS ‘just anxiety’?", "No, it’s a real disorder of the autonomic nervous system", ["Yes, always", "Only in young women", "It’s a made-up condition"],
          "Anxiety and POTS can coexist, but the fast heart rate on standing is measurable and physical."),
        Q("What is the outlook for most people with POTS?", "Many improve over a few years, especially after a viral trigger", ["It always gets worse", "It’s usually fatal", "It never changes"],
          "Treatment can make a big difference in the meantime."),
    ],
    [("What does POTS stand for?", "Postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome: a fast heart rate when upright, with symptoms such as dizziness, fatigue and brain fog."),
     ("Should I add salt if I think I have POTS?", "Check with your GP first, especially if you have high blood pressure, heart or kidney disease."),
     ("Is this quiz medical advice?", "No. It’s general education. See your GP for a proper assessment.")])

MG = C("myasthenia-gravis", "Myasthenia Gravis", "👁️",
    "Free myasthenia gravis quiz: 12 questions on fatigable weakness, droopy eyelids, the ice pack test, thymoma, crisis and drugs to avoid.",
    "Twelve questions, easy to hard, on myasthenia gravis: why muscles tire, the classic signs, tests, treatments and which medicines can make it worse.",
    [
        Q("What does myasthenia gravis attack?", "The connection between nerves and muscles", ["The spinal cord", "Muscle fibres directly", "The brain’s motor cortex"],
          "Antibodies block acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction, so signals don’t get through."),
        Q("What is the hallmark of myasthenia gravis?", "Weakness that gets worse with use and better with rest", ["Constant weakness that never changes", "Pain without weakness", "Numbness"],
          "‘Fatigable weakness’: worse at the end of the day or after repeated movements."),
        Q("What is the most common first sign?", "Droopy eyelids or double vision", ["Leg cramps", "Hearing loss", "A rash"],
          "Eye muscles are involved first in most people. In some, it stays only in the eyes."),
        Q("What bedside test can improve a droopy eyelid?", "An ice pack on the eyelid for 2 minutes", ["A hot water bottle", "Shining a torch", "Tapping the forehead"],
          "Cold improves transmission at the neuromuscular junction. A simple, cheap clue."),
        Q("Which tumour is linked to myasthenia gravis?", "Thymoma", ["Melanoma", "Lymphoma", "Meningioma"],
          "Everyone with MG gets a chest CT. Removing the thymus can help even without a tumour."),
        Q("What medicine boosts signals for symptom relief?", "Pyridostigmine", ["Pyrimethamine", "Prednisolone eye drops", "Propranolol"],
          "It slows breakdown of acetylcholine. Side effects: cramps, diarrhoea, drooling."),
        Q("In a myasthenic crisis, what is the best way to monitor breathing?", "Forced vital capacity (FVC), not just oxygen sats", ["Oxygen saturation only", "Chest X-ray", "Peak flow"],
          "Sats stay normal until very late. A falling FVC means the breathing muscles are tiring."),
        Q("Which medicines can worsen myasthenia gravis?", "Some antibiotics (aminoglycosides, quinolones), magnesium and beta blockers", ["Paracetamol", "Vitamin D", "Saline"],
          "Always tell prescribers you have MG. Many infections and medicines can trigger a flare."),
        Q("Which blood test helps confirm myasthenia gravis?", "Acetylcholine receptor antibodies", ["Rheumatoid factor", "Troponin", "Thyroid antibodies only"],
          "Positive in about 85% of generalised MG. Others have MuSK antibodies."),
        Q("A similar condition that improves with repeated use and is linked to lung cancer is called?", "Lambert–Eaton syndrome", ["Guillain–Barré syndrome", "Multiple sclerosis", "Motor neurone disease"],
          "Lambert–Eaton affects calcium channels and often comes with small cell lung cancer."),
        Q("What can happen to babies of mothers with myasthenia gravis?", "Temporary weakness from the mother’s antibodies", ["Lifelong MG always", "Nothing at all", "Deafness"],
          "Transient neonatal myasthenia affects about 1 in 10. It fades over weeks as antibodies clear."),
        Q("Which newer treatments target the antibodies themselves?", "FcRn blockers and complement inhibitors", ["Antibiotics", "Antivirals", "Statins"],
          "Steroids and other immunosuppressants are still the mainstay, but new options are changing care."),
    ],
    [("Is myasthenia gravis inherited?", "Usually not. It’s an autoimmune condition, though some rare congenital forms are genetic."),
     ("Can people with myasthenia gravis live normally?", "With treatment, most people have good control and a normal life expectancy."),
     ("Is this quiz medical advice?", "No. It’s general education. Talk to your doctor about your own care.")])

